Socioeconomic Deprivation Distress - Adjustment Disorder - V59.5 (Extreme poverty)
Diversity Equity and Inclusion
Housing/food insecurity hyperarousal, Scarcity mindset cognitive depletion, Financial emergency dread

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DEI-041
Reason for Visit: Acute hypertensive crisis and presyncope secondary to severe physiological exhaustion and sleep deprivation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute onset of severe dizziness upon standing, epistaxis, and a reported automated blood pressure reading of 190/110 following a 72-hour period of complete insomnia. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was required to definitively rule out a hypertensive emergency, stroke, or severe metabolic derangement prior to routine GP availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My nose started bleeding, my head is pounding, and I cannot sleep because if my car doesn't start tomorrow, I lose my job and my home.
History of Present Illness: The patient describes a chronic history of persistent physiological hyperarousal related to severe housing and food insecurity. Over the past three days, faced with an unexpected car repair bill, the patient developed pervasive financial emergency dread, leading to complete sleep deprivation caused by working multiple precarious low-wage jobs to cover the deficit. The patient reports chronic cognitive depletion (scarcity mindset) impairing long-term planning, leading to a recent delayed medical intervention due to the inability to afford copayments. Tonight, this chronic allostatic load manifested somatically as an acute epistaxis episode, severe headache, and presyncope. The patient exhibits intense shame when discussing the use of food banks and expresses deep generational despair stemming from the mathematical impossibility of upward mobility.
Functional Status: Acutely functionally impaired; unable to safely stand or operate machinery due to severe dizziness and physical exhaustion.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of preventable severe cardiovascular morbidity due to untreated essential hypertension driven entirely by extreme socioeconomic deprivation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 185/105 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Patient appears prematurely aged, profoundly exhausted, diaphoretic, and clutching a stack of unpaid bills.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia, regular rhythm, no murmurs. Marked hypertension. Respiratory: Clear breath sounds bilaterally. HEENT: Recent epistaxis resolved (clot visible in left anterior nare). Neurological: Alert and oriented, but exhibits noticeable cognitive slowing. Cranial nerves intact. No focal motor or sensory deficits. The clinical presentation is dominated by housing/food insecurity hyperarousal; the patient jumps at ambient noises outside the apartment. Scarcity mindset cognitive depletion is evident as the patient struggles to recall the names of their basic medications, displaying diminished executive functioning during this financial emergency.
Environmental Observations: The residence is sparsely furnished; the refrigerator is entirely empty except for condiments, and the heating is turned off despite cold ambient temperatures.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has been rationing over-the-counter paracetamol for headaches; no prescribed antihypertensives.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Socioeconomic Deprivation Distress - Adjustment Disorder - V59.5 (Extreme poverty) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Hypertensive Encephalopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of vomiting, visual disturbances, confusion, or focal neurological signs, despite the markedly elevated blood pressure.

Primary Insomnia - Ruled out/unlikely as the sleep deprivation is strictly secondary to the objective necessity of working multiple shifts to survive extreme poverty, rather than a primary sleep architecture disorder.

Acute Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to bilaterally equal motor strength, normal speech articulation, and intact cranial nerves.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with symptomatic, severe hypertension (BP 185/105), acute epistaxis, and resting tachycardia (HR 110) directly resulting from profound sleep deprivation and systemic socioeconomic stress. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was critical to perform a neurological examination, definitively exclude a hypertensive emergency or acute cerebrovascular event, and safely manage the blood pressure to prevent end-organ damage. Delaying care until routine hours would have left an exhausted, highly stressed patient at imminent risk for a catastrophic cardiovascular event, strictly satisfying the criteria for urgent medical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiated strict bed rest to naturally lower the blood pressure. Provided a 3-day medical certificate for immediate stress leave to halt the 72-hour work cycle. Advised the usual GP to review objective documentation of poverty on social determinants screening and assess physiological markers of premature cellular aging.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the acute physiological hyperarousal safely, implement a "financial compartmentalization protocol": place all bills and financial documents into a physical envelope and seal it, committing to only open it during a specific 30-minute window on Tuesday mornings; physically hiding the stressors reduces the continuous visual trigger for adrenaline release. To improve nutritional intake despite the scarcity mindset, commit to a "one warm meal" rule daily using the provided community pantry resources; checking off this daily nutritional goal provides a small, achievable psychological victory and stabilizes blood sugar.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to community social work for emergency financial relief, food bank delivery, and utility assistance.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review within 24 hours to monitor blood pressure, assess the need for pharmacological antihypertensives, and coordinate social work interventions.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or contact 000 if experiencing sudden numbness or weakness on one side of the body, slurred speech, sudden loss of vision, or crushing chest pain.

